# Phase Three — Final Source Plan

The Awtsmoos is not revealed by noise but by exact vessels that let more light arrive;
Awtsmoos.com becomes futuristic when every boundary is deliberate, every control alive.

## Production wave one: Apps route isolation

### Files to rewrite completely

- `geelooy/apps/styles/base.css`
  - replace naked `body` ownership with `body[data-geelooy-route="apps"]`;
  - route-scope section hierarchy selectors;
  - preserve current gradients, typography, and spacing.

- `geelooy/apps/styles/filter.css`
  - route-scope filter layout and controls;
  - preserve full-width, touch-height, and focus-visible behavior;
  - add `min-width: 0` where form tracks need spill protection.

- `geelooy/apps/styles/interactions.css`
  - route-scope every control selector;
  - preserve pointer-qualified hover, touch active, focus-visible, reduced motion.

- `geelooy/apps/styles/cards.css`
  - route-scope cards, icons, commerce badges, and transitions;
  - move hover transform under hover-capable fine-pointer media;
  - keep active and focus behavior available to keyboard/touch through their owning modules.

- `geelooy/apps/styles/hero.css`
  - route-scope hero, actions, note;
  - preserve fluid type and bounded grid tracks;
  - add containment-safe minimum widths only if required by current rules.

- `geelooy/apps/styles/discovery.css`
  - route-scope sticky filter, catalog status, cards, focus, hidden/reduced-motion rules;
  - retain local z-index 6 rather than escalating;
  - keep mobile one-column behavior.

- `geelooy/apps/styles/responsive.css`
  - route-scope all responsive selectors;
  - preserve mobile-first stacking, full-width actions, and reduced-motion behavior.

### Files intentionally unchanged in wave one

- `geelooy/apps/index.html`: markup already has a stable route root and semantic search structure.
- `geelooy/apps/scripts/apps-filter.js`: already tiny and documented.
- `geelooy/apps/scripts/filter/AppsFilterTiferesRuntime.js`: already class-based, abortable, snapshot-capable, and under the modular ceiling.
- Catalog data/renderer: preserve until a real UX/data gap is measured.
- Animator source: owned by a live concurrent final plan.

## Production wave two: risk tooling

Inspect existing `geelooy/tools`, `geelooy/tests`, and script helpers for a CSS/public-surface hygiene scanner. If absent, create a small read-only scanner split into focused modules under an existing verification area. It must never rewrite files automatically.

## Production wave three: evidence-selected surfaces

Run the scanner, rank public defects, then inspect the highest-risk pages completely before any rewrite. Favor high-reach route families and overlay/mobile hazards.

## Agent/docs continuation

Inspect canonical global docs/API discovery while Animator lands. After its agent facade is present, verify its manifest/install bridge and expose only truthful crosslinks from global docs/catalogs.

## Verification contract

After wave one source writes:
1. reread all seven CSS files completely;
2. verify every effective selector is Apps-route scoped;
3. verify no `100vw`, uncontrolled width, or extreme z-index was introduced;
4. browser-test Apps at 320px, 390px, and desktop;
5. keyboard-focus search/select/cards;
6. inspect console;
7. verify reduced-motion stylesheet path statically;
8. write planned-vs-actual delta;
9. only then perform a second source pass if evidence requires it.
